JSON.parse():


- A common use of JSON is to exchange data to/from a web server.
- ⚠️⚠️ When receiving data from a web server, the data is always a string.


#) Array as Object/ JSON Objects:
- 💡 Data written as an string of JSON object will be parsed into a JavaScript object

    <script>
        // Imagine we received this text from a web server: '{"name":"John", "age":30, "city":"New York"}'
        const text = '{"name":"John", "age":30, "city":"New York"}'
        const myObj = JSON.parse(text);
        document.getElementById("demo").innerHTML = myObj.name;   // John
    </script>

- ⚠️Make sure the text is in JSON format, or else you will get a syntax error.



#) Array as JSON/ JSON Arrays:
    - Data written as an string of JSON array will be parsed into a JavaScript array

        <script>
            // Imagine we received this text from a web server: '[ "Ford", "BMW", "Audi", "Fiat" ]'
            const text = '[ "Ford", "BMW", "Audi", "Fiat" ]';
            const myArr = JSON.parse(text);
            document.getElementById("demo").innerHTML = myArr[0];   // Ford
        </script>



#) Exceptions:
    
    1️⃣ Parsing Dates:
        - ❌ Date objects are not allowed in JSON.
        - ✅ If you need to include a date, write it as a string. You can convert it back into a date object
            later:
        - eg: Convert a string into a date:

            <script>
                const text = '{"name":"John", "birth":"1986-12-14", "city":"New York"}';
                const myObj = JSON.parse(text);
                myObj.birth = new Date(myObj.birth);
                document.getElementById("demo").innerHTML = myObj.name + ", " + myObj.birth; 
            </script>

            o/p:    John, Sun Dec 14 1986 05:45:00 GMT+0545 (Nepal Time)

        - ✅ Or, you can use the second parameter, of the JSON.parse() function, called reviver.
        - 💡 The reviver parameter is a function that checks each property, before returning the value.

            <script>
                const text = '{"name":"John", "birth":"1986-12-14", "city":"New York"}';
                const myObj = JSON.parse(text, 👉function (key, value) {
                    if (key == "birth") {
                        return new Date(value);
                    } else {
                        return value;
                    }
                });
                document.getElementById("demo").innerHTML = myObj.name + ", " + myObj.birth; 
            </script>

            o/p:    John, Sun Dec 14 1986 05:45:00 GMT+0545 (Nepal Time)
    
    2️⃣ Parsing Functions:
        - ❌ Functions are not allowed in JSON.
        - ✅ If you need to include a function, write it as a string. You can convert it back into a function
            later:

            <script>
                const text = '{"name":"John", "age":"function() {return 30;}", "city":"New York"}';
                const myObj = JSON.parse(text);
                obj.age = eval("(" + obj.age + ")");
                document.getElementById("demo").innerHTML = myObj.name + ", " + myObj.age(); 
            </script>

            o/p:    John, 30
        
        - 💀 You should avoid using functions in JSON, the functions will lose their scope, and you would
            have to use eval() to convert them back into functions.